{:edb/db-before-t 156 :edb/db-after-t 157 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533469 50 #inst "2026-10-11T01:12:20.336Z" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186064618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061755 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186064618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396654 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186064618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186064618 1071 "For the function $f(x) = x - 2\\ln x$, defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2} \\le x \\le e$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2} + 2\\ln 2$\n2. The global minimum value is $e - 2$\n3. The global minimum value is $2 - 2\\ln 2$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186064618 1071 "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le e,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$\nII. The global minimum value is $e-2$\nIII. The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186064618 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](images/6c/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396584 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397730 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186069374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067384 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186069374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397724 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186069374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186069374 1074 "The graph breaks at $x=-2$ and $x=0$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$ and $x=0.$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186078574 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397661 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397765 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397772 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186087885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397227 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186096638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396626 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186096638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397737 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186100924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396640 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186115340 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396619 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186115383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396808 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186115889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114241 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186115889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397718 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186115889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186115889 1071 "![](images/8e/8e7daf95fadbe2262a09e8183f00d63075655f3b97dd363984c192ac3414ad17.png)\n\nConsider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186115889 1071 "![](images/8e/8e7daf95fadbe2262a09e8183f00d63075655f3b97dd363984c192ac3414ad17.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186115889 1074 "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a jump discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=2$ the function has an infinite discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2.$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186119929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396591 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186119929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397751 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186119929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397758 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186124480 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397744 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121762 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121766 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121770 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121774 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397509 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397513 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397517 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397521 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1071 "$$\nx(x-3)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=2$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1071 "$x(x - 3)(x + 1)y^{″} + (x - 3)y^{′} + x(x + 1)(\\ln x)y = 0$\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.The point $x =-1$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\nThe point $x = 0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\nThe point $x = 2$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\nThe point $x = 3$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186124867 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-3)(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-3)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x-3=0$ or where $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is undefined. These occur at $x=3$ and $x\\le 0.$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ but the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=3,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-3)P(x)=(x-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=3,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}Q(x)=(x-3)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}=(x-3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=3.$ Therefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186147505 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396577 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186147512 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397389 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186147512 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397442 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186147512 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397456 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186147535 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397538 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397028 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397038 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397099 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397113 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397127 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397290 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397310 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149919 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149922 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149925 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149928 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397048 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397052 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397055 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397058 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″}(x) + x^{2}y^{′}(x) + y(x) = 2x, 0 \\le x \\le π$\n\n$2y(0) + y^{′}(0) = 0, y(π) + 2y^{′}(π) = 0$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\nThe governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\nFor this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\nThe boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186152581 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)=2x≢0,$ the equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Robin}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is inhomogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149932 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149935 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149938 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149942 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397061 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397065 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397068 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397071 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″}(x) + \\sin (x)y^{′}(x) + 3y(x) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le 2$\n\n$y^{′}(0) = 2, y^{′}(2) =-1$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\nThe governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\nFor this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\nThe boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186152582 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Neumann}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186157460 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396723 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186157460 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396749 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186157462 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396937 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186157462 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396956 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186157462 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397671 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186157462 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397690 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186157463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396973 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186157463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396992 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186157463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397011 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186166942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396903 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186166945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397141 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186166945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397154 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186166945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397183 1319