[17592186222031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186222031 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,3) \\\\ -3x, & x\\in [-3,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186222031 1071 "$F(x) = {x^{2}, \\mid x ∈ [0, 3); - 3x, \\mid x ∈ [- 3, 0)$\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x ∈ [- 3, 3)$. Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x ∈ R$.\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x ∈ (-3, 3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x ∈ (-3, 3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $lim_(x → - 3^{+})F(x) = lim_(x → 3^{-})F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186222031 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/186106373f9848cfefefb23197e7508497ff3d570f326da2ca342d078682128d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}-3x=-3(-3)=9\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2}=(3)^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/89/89c3a597ce1114fee9e775f291cf89e8ca04f61eb39cf2b4c896dd4be8022f09.png)" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396660 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396666 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396686 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396702 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229151 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396867 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229151 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396924 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397707 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397080 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397208 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397271 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397419 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229176 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397489 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397412 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397502 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397167 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397239 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397255 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228537 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186229727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228540 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186229727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397074 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397077 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229727 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = x\\cos (x)$ on $x ∈ [- π, π]$. Select the correct options below.\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\nConsequently, the\n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186229727 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229727 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396578 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1069 "q-103178" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1071 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nI. $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$\nII. $xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$\nIII. $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1072 #uuid "8e4d2f85-2640-5963-a9fc-d023ea35768b" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$  gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''III only''." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396579 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396580 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396581 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396582 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396583 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1077 "selection" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396579 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1080 #uuid "c0ee641f-c56c-5e39-8179-c1c054897293" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396579 1081 "ÍII only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396579 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396579 1084 #uuid "65cb7b3b-ca02-57e0-a31c-2311ebcd6860" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396580 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396580 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396580 1084 #uuid "21a7e756-add5-5d08-a0b6-865f25787d6d" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396581 1081 "I only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396581 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396581 1084 #uuid "0417384c-c1ac-529e-bf13-223231a54322" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396582 1081 "I and II only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396582 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396582 1084 #uuid "4737d57e-814c-5ac7-acfb-48ce13b5a3e9" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396583 1081 "II only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396583 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396583 1084 #uuid "95ce12e4-12c0-5c3c-9bc0-e72604c4d3e6" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396585 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1069 "q-104025" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1071 "![](images/2d/2d342689f9e4a213ac5407ccc132868b51ba218afabda1a871e7ca7b02345a17.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1072 #uuid "c6685bf3-2db8-58de-88bd-a80799c06e2f" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1074 "The graph breaks at $x=-1$ and $x=2,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396586 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396587 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396588 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396589 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396590 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1077 "selection" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396587 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1080 #uuid "53daf543-087e-50bb-928d-d5b44cae5b1c" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396586 1081 "$x=2$ only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396586 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396586 1084 #uuid "54ba84a1-3d25-5cd1-98bf-f731c2622e1d" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396587 1081 "$x=-1$ and $x=2$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396587 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396587 1084 #uuid "da75cf17-b00a-5ed0-97c9-6265dae86079" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396588 1081 "$x=1$ and $x=2$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396588 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396588 1084 #uuid "77df7b4b-c87e-5e29-808b-f38574caacd1" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396589 1081 "$x=-2$ and $x=1$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396589 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396589 1084 #uuid "8c127ea3-d160-5664-896d-8df951530b12" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396590 1081 "$x=-1$ only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396590 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396590 1084 #uuid "d02a6f82-debb-558b-a876-dc02aab50551" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396592 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1069 "q-105291" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1071 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1072 #uuid "faa7ddb4-5b88-5e56-a53e-35fdb9d2ef93" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396593 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396594 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396595 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396596 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396597 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1077 "selection" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396596 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1080 #uuid "a01010bc-5e11-583a-98fc-7b20994c0fc5" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396593 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396593 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396593 1084 #uuid "e5f3b240-c702-580d-9cdf-43123253f773" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396594 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396594 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396594 1084 #uuid "574dea89-ee9d-5556-a72b-c257c732699e" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396595 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396595 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396595 1084 #uuid "640a91d9-57f7-5e99-bc15-29e1456e8af0" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396596 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396596 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396596 1084 #uuid "2044901c-e971-534a-af35-fba5ec803324" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396597 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396597 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396597 1084 #uuid "3bf8a5fb-65f3-58d7-a288-3f0a87f9681a" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396599 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1069 "q-106929" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1071 "Let $Y$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in R$ with coefficients in $R$ of degree exactly $2.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1072 #uuid "4c206776-99db-55ba-ae48-7c0ec7d6d597" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **addition of polynomials in $Y$ is not closed.** *\n\nIndeed, let's consider, for example, the polynomials $p(x)={x}^{2}+x$ and $q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$ Then $p,q\\in Y,$ but \n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)={x}^{2}+x+(-{x}^{2})=x\\notin Y\n$$\n\n because $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)=1\\ne 2.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- $1\\cdot p=p$ for all $p\\in Y.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\,✓$" 13194139533469 