39533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396972 1084 #uuid "cfba429b-73d7-56fd-a05b-13890a45950f" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396974 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396979 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396985 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396973 1069 "q-337466" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396973 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396973 1072 #uuid "60c74d98-62e0-535f-ae2c-bd22a02ff3d1" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396973 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396974 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396975 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396974 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396976 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396974 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396977 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396974 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396978 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396974 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396974 1077 "field-3" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396974 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396978 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396974 1080 #uuid "459bee54-35cb-58cd-8bd7-d48f32a50b85" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396975 1081 "x{e}^{4x}" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396975 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396975 1084 #uuid "544ef45e-9801-59af-90c5-d2e5923d0da8" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396976 1081 "{e}^{4x}" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396976 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396976 1084 #uuid "7e87c929-0475-5c78-9a67-dac2d411868c" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396977 1081 "{e}^{-4x}" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396977 1083 #edb/re