}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-m-2 & =0 \\\\ (m-2)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=2$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{2} & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={e}^{-1}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397081 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397082 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397081 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397083 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397081 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397084 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397081 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397081 1077 "field-4" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397081 1079 #edb/ref 17592186397082 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397081 1080 #uuid "e847dcfa-8d4c-527b-a527-764fa7af066c" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397082 1081 "has the unique solution $y≡0$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397082 1083 #edb/ref 