VP {{field-4}}." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397208 1072 #uuid "76fbcb9f-680c-51b9-b052-efdce55007dc" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397208 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$   \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Robin boundary conditions given by\n\n$$\ny(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, setting ${\\alpha }_{1}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{1}=0,$ ${\\alpha }_{2}=0,$ ${\\beta }_{2}=1,$ and $a=1,$ $b=3,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}(3) & {y}_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-4m+3 & =0 \\\\ (m-1)(m-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have ${m}_{1}=1$ and ${m}_{2}=3,$ and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1} & {e}^{3(1)} \\\\ {e}^{3} & 3{e}^{3(3)}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}e & {e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{3} & 3{e}^{9}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =e(3{e}^{9})-{e}^{3}({e}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{10}-{e}^{6} \\\\  & ={e}^{6}(3{e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397209 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397210 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397209 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397211 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397209 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397212 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397209 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397209 1077 "field-4" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397209 1079 #edb/ref 17592186397210 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397209 1080 #uuid "0c637c93-1ee3-58ef-993c-3c6e5c1fc84b" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397210 1081 "has the unique solution $y≡0$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397210 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397210 1084 #uuid "fc9b72e9-fff2-5f3d-af71-a6cc5a8690e6" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397211 1081 "has infinitely many solutions" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397211 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397211 1084 #uuid "e0887495-5b99-519d-8b06-b050d9a2f259" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397212 1081 "has no solution" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397212 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397212 1084 #uuid "7ecfede9-c62d-5886-a299-f1a637666f1c" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397213 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397214 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397213 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397215 13194139533469 1