ewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397525 1072 #uuid "24b6f004-4c8b-5565-a4ef-1d172930ca34" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397525 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because $\\frac{1}{x}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e5eec10427a7061a05c0bd1228ce1146234bf2b09d75bb86ba83b28fbe178d9.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+2)=-2+2=0\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/4a/4afcdf86f777b9c7afc8af31b36cde4b80cfe394fd908db1ccd47ef48dff1617.png)" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397526 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397527 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397526 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397528 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397526 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397526 1077 "field-2" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397526 1079 #edb/ref 17592186397528 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397526 1080 #uuid "f2cb40da-a7f4-5c99-8c06-a63a258898b3" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397527 1081 "True" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397527 1083 #e