 true] [17592186397706 1084 #uuid "e2ff85d2-c6bc-5e83-bcd4-df22d2bc5e11" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397708 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397713 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397707 1069 "q-365387" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397707 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397707 1072 #uuid "01283c40-bda7-59b4-8f16-b320c423d254" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397707 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{t})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{x}{{t}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{t} & =-\\frac{x}{{t}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{t} & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{{t}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t({t}^{2}-1)}{{t}^{2}-x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t({t}^{2}-1)}{{t}^{2}-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{t({t}^{2}-1)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} \\\\ t({t}^{2}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={t}^{2}-x \\\\ t({t}^{2}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}.\n$$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397708 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397709 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397708 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397710 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397708 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397711 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186397708 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397712 1